#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a891eaf9-6a4c-5e87-8aa0-95920347bd4d" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Product as a Repeated Addition"}] :question/id #uuid "56936482-95f9-54ff-b006-cd79247947ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-13618" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd41e0c4d98c28e41c8e91743c70bbcac3e0b88071d1075e1cd518d2410843fa.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Write down this multiplication as a repeated addition of fraction models." :question/worked-solution "In the model, we have $\\dfrac{\\color{red}4}{5}$ multiplied by ${\\color{blue}3}.$ This means that we need to make $\\color{blue}3$ copies of the given fraction model.\n\n![](images/2a/2ab193d9bb045165bcfffadae012523f33297548eacbdfd6b3514c791d7fac58.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the missing picture is:\n\n![](images/59/59f5625dce754f49724d16b00e839ec34e473601df4702db51f3973f14fd4c10.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0abaf0f6-4018-54b2-a118-f420a3e0705c" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number Using Repeated Addition"}] :question/id #uuid "6a8062c4-e4e5-5045-8b33-7f0afaf76d72" :question/math-academy-id "e-13624" :question/problem "![](images/15/1553e601de2b1664996bb27d49cb09d3baacc72772f9846b45dc45dc9114ff53.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\dfrac{5}{6} \\times 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we need to multiply $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{6}$ by $\\color{blue}3$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b9443ceedbda3d541b0fc4d69489eead83dc8550710c7b6b3f1f9ed90d1dc97b.png)\n\n \n\nThere are ${\\color{red}5} \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 15$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} \\times 3 = \\dfrac{15}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78c0c75e-8a40-5064-a62a-954d8b85c3c1" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number by Repeating the Shaded Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "3c170236-340b-5446-960f-a20b6937c23c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13628" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a7e53c31ac53ee142500771bf19af2995c02ecb6fef9aa37e73402e63fa7d518.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the multiplication problem above?" :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{3}{7}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{6}{7}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/a7/a7b6a481c90b7372d0b78f7029b3fe8ae9c9c98f484459e1b7697308e3e2ebce.png)\n\nThe shape on the left has $3$ shaded pieces, and the shape on the right has $6$ shaded pieces. So the number of shaded pieces has increased by a factor of ${\\color{blue}2}.$ (That is, the number of shaded pieces has been multiplied by ${\\color{blue}2}.$)\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{7} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = \\dfrac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a891eaf9-6a4c-5e87-8aa0-95920347bd4d" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Product as a Repeated Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52426d8a-7df6-5d0b-8df7-eaba47f045f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d36baecd03abcda135ef9b7363bdce93e352e6d5676513109c368da85c3623f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425424} {:answer/id #uuid "5dba6e23-e3d4-5cb9-a1c1-ff5011bf81d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04bf457299d2d7d4f45118e56acce8888b765c70a620cd65fdf6589687638746.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425431} {:answer/id #uuid "7116d3a3-9611-5f92-ae23-c110e7f2a174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/75/755cfb432c